.TH smi-jenkinsfile "1" "August 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-jenkinsfile \- Run a Jenkins declarative Jenkinsfile locally, without Jenkins
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-jenkinsfile
[\fB\-b\fR \fIBRANCH\fR]
[\fB\-e\fR \fIKEY\fR=\fIVALUE\fR]...
[\fIJENKINSFILE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-jenkinsfile
executes a Jenkins declarative pipeline on the local machine.
The Jenkinsfile is compiled as a Groovy script against a small pipeline base class,
which implements the declarative DSL with closure delegates: every directive
.RB ( pipeline ,
.BR agent ,
.BR triggers ,
.BR options ,
.BR environment ,
.BR stages ,
.BR stage ,
.BR when ,
.BR steps ,
.BR parallel ,
.BR post )
is a method call on a block object that collects a model, and the model is then
executed.
.PP
The directory containing the Jenkinsfile is used as the workspace.
It is a deliberately small implementation, meant for trying out a Jenkinsfile and for
understanding how the DSL works, not a Jenkins replacement.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-b\  BRANCH \fR,\fB\ \-\-branch= BRANCH
Value of
.BR env.BRANCH_NAME .
Default is
.BR master .
This is what the
.B when
conditions of the pipeline are evaluated against.
.TP
.BI \-e\  KEY = VALUE \fR,\fB\ \-\-env= KEY = VALUE
Set an environment variable.
It wins over the
.B environment
block, so a run can be steered without editing the Jenkinsfile.
Repeat the option for more variables.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.TP
.I JENKINSFILE
Jenkinsfile to run.
Default is
.B ./Jenkinsfile
in the current directory.
.SH STEPS
The following steps are implemented:
.PP
.BR echo ,\  sh ,\  bat ,\  isUnix ,\  script ,\  fileExists ,\  error ,\  sleep ,
.BR retry ,\  timeout ,\  emailext .
.PP
.B sh
runs the script with
.BR "sh \-xe" :
every expanded command is echoed, and a multi\-line script stops at its first failing
line, as it does on Jenkins.
.B bat
runs it with
.BR "cmd /c" .
Both start a real process in the workspace, with the pipeline environment passed to it,
so shell expansion of environment variables works.
A non\-zero exit code fails the build.
.PP
.B emailext
prints the message that Jenkins would send instead of sending it.
.PP
Any other step is reported as not implemented and skipped: it does not fail the build.
.PP
.B timeout
does not interrupt its body; the duration is checked after the body has finished.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The environment of the calling shell is inherited first, so
.B PATH
and everything else on the machine is available to the pipeline and to its
.B sh
steps.
A Jenkinsfile that extends it, as in
.PP
.RS
.B environment { PATH = "/opt/setmy.info/bin:$PATH" }
.RE
.PP
therefore works the same way it does on a Jenkins agent.
On top of that the following variables are provided before the Jenkinsfile is read:
.BR NODE_NAME ,\  WORKSPACE ,\  JOB_NAME ,\  BUILD_NUMBER ,\  BUILD_URL ,
.BR BRANCH_NAME ,\  GIT_BRANCH ,\  GIT_URL .
.TP
.B SMI_JENKINSFILE_DEPLOY
Runs the steps of the
.B Publish
and
.B Deploy
stages.
.BR true ,\  yes ,\  on \  or \  1
switches them on; any other value, including unset, switches them off.
Off by default.
Stages switched off are reported as
.BR BLOCKED .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The pipeline finished with result SUCCESS.
.TP
.B 1
The pipeline finished with result FAILURE, or the Jenkinsfile was not found.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Run the Jenkinsfile of the current directory as branch master:
.PP
.RS
.B smi-jenkinsfile
.RE
.PP
Run another Jenkinsfile as a hotfix branch:
.PP
.RS
.B smi-jenkinsfile \-b hotfix/NPE ../jenkinsfile-starter/Jenkinsfile
.RE
.PP
Run the master branch, but without the live deployment:
.PP
.RS
.B smi-jenkinsfile \-e MASTER_TO_LIVE=SKIP
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jenkins-cli (1),
.BR smi-ci-cli (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Imre Tabur
